{#
  ONE definition of a sub-page hero's responsive URLs, called by BOTH the <img>
  element and by head.njk's preload.

  This exists because the element's `srcset` and the preload's `imagesrcset`
  have to be byte-identical or the preload fetches a candidate the element never
  uses -- highest-priority bytes nothing draws, and the hero that DOES draw gets
  no preload at all. head.njk's own comment records that regression happening
  here on 2026-09-02, when a hand-written preload path and the field that
  rendered the background drifted apart. Written once and imported twice, they
  cannot disagree.

  `heroWidths`, `heroDims` and `heroAlt` are filters, not bare interpolations,
  and all three THROW rather than rendering something empty: an unknown stem
  emits srcset="", and a missing alt emits alt="", which is not "no alt" but a
  positive claim that a 60svh photograph is decorative. See .eleventy.js.
#}

{%- macro heroSrcset(stem) -%}
{%- for w in stem | heroWidths -%}/img/{{ stem }}-{{ w }}w.webp {{ w }}w{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{%- endfor -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro heroLargest(stem) -%}
/img/{{ stem }}-{{ (stem | heroWidths) | last }}w.webp
{%- endmacro -%}

{#
  The hero itself. Doc 10 s2.3: text never sits on photography EXCEPT the
  sub-page hero <h1>, which sits on a bottom-third scrim, left-aligned at the
  gutter. s5.3 names the component and its 60svh height.

  `object-position` is NOT a parameter here. It is a per-page CSS override keyed
  off the page wrapper's existing id (see styles.css), because an inline
  `style=` attribute would need a CSP hash and the whole point of replacing the
  old `style="background-image: url(...)"` divs with an <img> was to leave CSP
  untouched -- and to carry alt text those divs never had.

  THERE IS NO `.reveal` ON THIS BLOCK, and its removal is a fix rather than an
  omission (behavioural eval B2/B3, 2026-09-06). It carried one, which meant the
  h1 and the subtitle were hidden until the script added `.seen`: with
  JavaScript blocked, or with JS running but no IntersectionObserver, all seven
  pages rendered the photograph, the gradient and the nav with NO TITLE AT ALL.

  Reveal-gating body content below the fold is this site's established pattern
  and is fine. The page's IDENTITY is not body content: before this relay the
  <h1> was plain text in a black band and was immune, and putting it inside a
  gated block was a regression the pattern does not excuse. It also cost 300ms
  on a fast connection and up to 750ms throttled before the title reached full
  opacity, re-ran the fade on Back, and slid the line 28px while it faded.

  The photograph was already static and first-paint -- "it's a photograph, not a
  rig" (doc 10 s5.3) -- so the whole hero is now first-paint, which is what that
  sentence was always describing. No hold: heldTargets() in js/animations.js is
  page-home only and stays so. `prefers-reduced-motion` is unaffected because
  there is no longer any motion here to reduce.
#}
{%- macro heroSub(stem, alt, heading, subtitle) -%}
<section class="hero-sub">
    <img src="{{ heroLargest(stem) }}" srcset="{{ heroSrcset(stem) }}" sizes="100vw"
         alt="{{ alt | heroAlt }}" class="hero-sub__image"
         fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"
         width="{{ (stem | heroDims).w }}" height="{{ (stem | heroDims).h }}">
    <div class="hero-sub__content">
        <h1>{{ heading }}</h1>
        <p>{{ subtitle }}</p>
    </div>
</section>
{%- endmacro -%}
